<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aboriginal Tasmanians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Palawa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aboriginal Tasmanians are the Indigenous people of Tasmania, isolated from the mainland when rising seas formed Bass Strait about 10,000 years ago. Numbering several thousand at British settlement in 1803, they were devastated by violence, disease and dispossession, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-war">Black War</a> of the 1820s, and the survivors were removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flinders-island">Flinders Island</a>. Their descendants, many from the Bass Strait islands, form today's Tasmanian Aboriginal community.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians.jpg" alt="Aboriginal Tasmanians" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Duterrau (1768–1851) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Last_of_the_Tasmanians_Woodcut_6_-_Wooreddy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Aboriginal Tasmanians crossed into what is now Tasmania about 35,000 years ago, in the Late Pleistocene, when low sea levels in the Last Glacial Period exposed the Bassian Plain, a broad land bridge to the mainland. Rising seas later formed Bass Strait and left the island's communities isolated from the other Aboriginal groups of the mainland.</p><p>Before British colonisation in 1803 they kept distinct languages, cultural traditions and regional identities, organised in family groups and clans tied to particular territories and seasonal movements. A clan had a name of its own and comprised 40 to 50 people. They were mainly nomadic, following the seasons' supplies of seafood, land mammals, vegetables and berries. Estimates of the population before colonisation generally range from 3,000 to 15,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>British and American sealers began visiting the Bass Strait islands in the late 1790s, and the first significant European presence on the main island was a small military outpost at Risdon Cove in September 1803. Conflict came in phases. Between 1803 and 1808 it concerned common food sources such as oysters and kangaroos, and from 1808 to 1823 farmers, sealers and whalers traded in and abducted Aboriginal women. Disease also took a toll; historians differ over how soon and how far, with Geoffrey Blainey and Keith Windschuttle naming introduced disease as the main cause of the decline.</p><p>Pastoral expansion and the loss of game brought armed resistance from 1824. In 1826 the government gazette began to report acts of atrocity, and a proclamation of 1828 established military posts and then declared martial law. Aboriginal people needed passes to cross settled districts, and bounties of £5 for an adult and £2 for a child were offered for those captured without one, which often led to organised hunts.</p><p>Between 1830 and 1835 most survivors were removed to the Wybalenna Aboriginal Establishment on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flinders-island">Flinders Island</a>. The first 51 had been brought in late 1831 to The Lagoons, which was exposed and had little water, and children aged 6 to 15 were taken from their families. In October 1847 the 47 survivors were moved to Oyster Cove.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Colonial writers and popular media wrongly described Aboriginal Tasmanians as extinct after the deaths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truganini">Truganini</a> in 1876 and Fanny Cochrane Smith in 1905, each presented as the last. The community survived through descendants of Aboriginal women and European sealers in the Bass Strait islands, and thousands of people in Tasmania identify as Aboriginal Tasmanian. Historians continue to debate whether the events of colonisation amounted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> under the United Nations Genocide Convention.</p><p>The original languages are no longer spoken fluently. They survive in fragmentary wordlists recorded in inconsistent spellings by visitors and colonists, from which Palawa communities have built palawa kani, a reconstructed composite language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aboriginal Tasmanians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wider names are also used: Koori in New South Wales and Victoria, Murri in Queensland, Anangu in the central deserts, <b>Palawa</b> in Tasmania.</blockquote><a href="/culture/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Black War of the 1820s colonists and soldiers killed many <b>Aboriginal Tasmanians</b>, and the survivors were removed to Wybalenna on Flinders Island in the 1830s.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-tasmania">Colony of Tasmania</a></li></ul></section>
